From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:53:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb: Keep plain $document URL filters out of the content
 blocker

Malware lists are made of filters that block pages at one URL or under
one host, and nothing else: `||host^$document` and
`||host/path^$document`. The Online Malicious URL Blocklist, which
uBlock Origin enables by default, has about 98,000 of them. In the
adblock engine each takes over 100 bytes, so that one list costs about
17 MiB in every WebContent process, which builds its own engine, and
about 45 MiB more while the engine is being built.

Keep those filters as sorted 64-bit hashes of their patterns instead,
8 bytes each, and check a page against them as the engine would match
the filters: the filter's host is whole labels of the page's host (for
`||host^`, a suffix), and its path follows that host up to a separator
or the end of the URL. A hit is then handed to the engine as a match it
already found, so that exceptions still let the page through, and a
`$badfilter` for one of these filters drops its hash.

While here, stop copying every list twice more than needed when
building the engine, and skip the lines without a `#` when looking for
generic cosmetic selector lists, since those are all network filters.
